DP  - 2025 Nov
TI  - Case 343: Kaposiform Lymphangiomatosis.
PG  - e250975
LID - 10.1148/radiol.250975 [doi]
AB  - A 17-year-old boy presented to the outpatient department with left-sided breast 
      enlargement for 2 years. He also had multiple long bone fractures after trivial 
      trauma over the past 8 years, along with intermittent episodes of streaky 
      hemoptysis. He was treated with bisphosphonates, with marginal relief of 
      symptoms. At the index presentation, as part of preanesthetic checkup for 
      elective surgery for gynecomastia, laboratory investigations revealed 
      thrombocytopenia, with a platelet count of 100 000/µL (reference range, 
      150 000-400 000/µL), and a normal hormonal profile. Chest radiography was 
      performed, followed by diagnostic thoracentesis, which yielded minimal 
      hemorrhagic fluid. Subsequently, contrast-enhanced CT and contrast-enhanced MRI 
      were performed. Echocardiography showed diffuse pericardial thickening.
