DP  - 2026 Jul 10
TI  - Role of Point-of-care Ultrasound in Respiratory Medicine.
LID - 10.4103/aam.aam_416_26 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Point-of-care ultrasound (POCUS) has rapidly evolved as a 
      transformative bedside imaging modality in respiratory medicine. Unlike 
      conventional radiological investigations, POCUS provides real-time, dynamic 
      assessment of pulmonary and pleural pathology without exposure to ionizing 
      radiation. Its increasing adoption across emergency, critical care, and 
      outpatient settings reflects its clinical versatility and diagnostic accuracy. In 
      recent years, POCUS has gained particular importance in resource-limited 
      environments, where access to advanced imaging modalities such as computed 
      tomography may be restricted. OBJECTIVE: To comprehensively review the role, 
      applications, diagnostic utility, and limitations of POCUS in respiratory 
      medicine, with emphasis on its integration into routine clinical practice. 
      METHODOLOGY: A narrative review was conducted through a structured search of 
      electronic databases, including PubMed, Scopus, and Google Scholar. Literature 
      published in English was identified using keywords such as "point-of-care 
      ultrasound," "lung ultrasound," "respiratory diseases," "pleural disorders," and 
      "bedside imaging." Relevant clinical studies, guidelines, systematic reviews, and 
      narrative reviews focusing on diagnostic and procedural applications of POCUS 
      were included. Articles lacking clinical relevance or focusing exclusively on 
      nonrespiratory applications were excluded. The selected literature was 
      synthesized qualitatively to highlight key diagnostic principles, clinical 
      applications, and practical considerations. RESULTS: POCUS has demonstrated high 
      diagnostic accuracy in a wide range of respiratory conditions, including pleural 
      effusion, pneumothorax, pneumonia, interstitial lung disease, and pulmonary 
      edema. It allows rapid differentiation of causes of acute respiratory failure and 
      facilitates early clinical decision-making. Lung ultrasound artifacts such as 
      A-lines, B-lines, and consolidation patterns provide important diagnostic clues. 
      POCUS also plays a crucial role in guiding procedures such as thoracentesis, 
      thereby improving safety and reducing complications. In comparison to chest 
      radiography, it offers superior sensitivity in detecting pleural and parenchymal 
      abnormalities, particularly in critically ill patients. Despite its advantages, 
      limitations include operator dependency, a learning curve, and challenges in 
      standardization across different clinical settings. CONCLUSION: POCUS is an 
      indispensable and rapidly expanding tool in respiratory medicine, offering a 
      safe, cost-effective, and efficient alternative to conventional imaging. Its 
      integration into clinical practice enhances diagnostic precision, supports 
      bedside decision-making, and improves procedural safety. Wider adoption, 
      supported by structured training and standardized protocols, is essential to 
      fully realize its potential in both high-resource and resource-constrained 
      settings.
