TI  - Pancreaticopleural fistula: you can only find it if you look for it.
LID - 10.17235/reed.2024.10957/2024 [doi]
AB  - We present a case of a 52-year-old man with chronic toxic pancreatitis and 
      history of multiple hospital admissions secondary to pancreatitis exacerbation. 
      Due to persistent respiratory symptoms a thoracoabdominal computerized tomography 
      (CT) was performed. The CT showed a new significant right pleural effusion as 
      well as inflammatory changes secondary to acute pancreatitis. Within these 
      inflammatory changes extending towards the right diaphragmatic crura. The 
      diagnostic thoracentesis showed an amylase level of 4000UI/L. Subsequently, given 
      de suspicion of pancreaticopleural (PPF) fistula treatment with somatostatin 
      6mg/12h was initiated and endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) 
      was performed. The pancreatic duct could not be cannulated by CPRE so an 
      echoendoscopy was carried out with insertion of a pancreatic stent by pancreatic 
      rendez-vous technique. The evolution was satisfactory with resolution of the 
      fistula.
