TI  - Case Report of a Giant Mediastinal Neurofibroma Misdiagnosed as Massive Pleural 
      Effusion.
PG  - e71445
LID - 10.1002/ccr3.71445 [doi]
LID - e71445
AB  - Mediastinal neurogenic tumors are benign neoplasms commonly found along nerves in 
      the posterior mediastinum, with rare malignant transformation. We report a case 
      of a 36-year-old woman admitted to the respiratory department with complaints of 
      chest tightness, cough, and sputum production. Chest CT revealed a large 
      hypodense lesion in the left thoracic cavity with scattered streaky hyperdense 
      shadows, initially suspected as massive pleural effusion. Thoracentesis failed to 
      aspirate fluid. Subsequent ultrasound and contrast-enhanced CT identified a giant 
      mediastinal mass, prompting referral to thoracic surgery for resection. The tumor 
      was successfully removed, and postoperative pathology confirmed neurofibroma. The 
      patient recovered well and was discharged. For unexplained hypodense lesions in 
      the thoracic cavity, ultrasound and contrast-enhanced CT aid in reducing 
      misdiagnosis. For large intrathoracic tumors, successful resection requires 
      thorough preoperative evaluation, preparation, and optimal surgical access to 
      ensure safe and complete removal.
