DP  - 2025 Jul
TI  - Aorto-Atrial Fistula Case Report with Discussion on Gerbode Defect.
PG  - 295-297
AB  - This case report describes a 62-year-old female with a history of heart failure 
      with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF) who presented to the emergency 
      department with heart failure exacerbation. She was initially diagnosed with 
      bilateral pleural effusions and treated with thoracentesis and diuretics. When 
      follow-up indicated a lack of improvement, an ischemic workup was ordered. A 
      transesophageal echocardiogram (TEE) revealed an abnormal connection between the 
      right atrium and proximal aorta (aorto-atrial fistula), which was promptly fixed 
      with surgical patching. This report also discusses the Gerbode defect, an 
      abnormal congenital or acquired connection between the left ventricle and right 
      atrium. Both conditions can significantly impact patients undergoing anesthesia. 
      Further research on these abnormalities is essential to prevent poor outcomes.
