TI  - Spontaneous regression of pulmonary pleomorphic carcinoma after thoracoscopic 
      surgery for hemothorax: A case report.
PG  - 102147
LID - 10.1016/j.rmcr.2024.102147 [doi]
LID - 102147
AB  - A 69-year-old man visited our hospital due to left pleural effusion by chest 
      X-ray. Thoracentesis indicated hemothorax and the specimen of pleural effusion 
      confirmed malignant cells. Because anemia became apparent due to persistent 
      bloody pleural effusion, thoracoscopic hemostasis was performed with surgical 
      biopsy for a tumor extending beyond the visceral pleural of the left upper lobe 
      and the patient was finally diagnosed as having a primary pleomorphic carcinoma. 
      Then, the tumor rapidly shrunk and completely resolved in three months. Our case 
      indicates that spontaneous regression possibly develops following thoracoscopic 
      surgery for hemothorax in pulmonary pleomorphic carcinoma patients.
