TI  - Optimizing anxiolysis and analgesia for percutaneous intervention by the 
      abdominal radiologist.
PG  - 2721-2729
LID - 10.1007/s00261-022-03417-4 [doi]
AB  - Abdominal radiologists perform a wide variety of image-guided interventions. 
      Procedures performed by abdominal radiologists can be broadly categorized into 
      paracentesis, thoracentesis, superficial and deep soft tissue biopsy, drain 
      placement, and ablation. As these procedures continue to develop as an 
      alternative to more invasive and potentially morbid interventions, and with 
      continued improvements in minimally invasive technologies, it becomes 
      increasingly important for abdominal radiologists to be familiar with options for 
      peri-procedural analgesia and anxiolysis, as well as when to consult 
      anesthesiology. In this review, we discuss analgesic, anxiolytic, and 
      nonpharmacologic options available to the abdominal radiologist. We focus on 
      practical agents that are relatively safe for general use, special populations, 
      and considerations for post-procedural monitoring.
