TI  - Acute Tension Hydrothorax in a Cirrhotic Patient With Hepatic Hydrothorax Without 
      Ascites.
PG  - e13941
LID - 10.7759/cureus.13941 [doi]
LID - e13941
AB  - We describe a case of a 50-year-old man with alcohol cirrhosis status post 
      transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS) who presented with dyspnea, 
      refractory hepatic hydrothorax (HH), and no ascites who subsequently developed 
      acute tension hydrothorax (TH). Urgent ultrasound-guided thoracentesis was 
      performed with a significant improvement of symptoms. Further management 
      consisted of a chest tube placement, subsequently removed with a plan for 
      intermittent thoracentesis as needed, diuretic therapy, and salt restriction. HH 
      occurs in 5%-10% of patients with cirrhosis, and TH in these patients is a rare 
      entity that requires prompt recognition and drainage as it may be 
      life-threatening.
