VI  - 18
IP  - 3
DP  - 2022 May-Jun
TI  - Undiagnosed pleural effusion treated with traditional Chinese medicine: A case 
      report.
PG  - 362-365
LID - S1550-8307(21)00041-0 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.explore.2021.02.005 [doi]
AB  - The main clinical manifestations of pleural effusion are exertional dyspnea, 
      predominantly dry cough, and pleuritic chest pain. To treat pleural effusion 
      appropriately, it is important to determine its etiology; which however, remains 
      unclear in nearly 20% of cases.A 73-year-old man with a history of invasive 
      pulmonary tuberculosis (TB), had been experiencing chest congestion and dyspnea 
      with undiagnosed pleural effusion for six years. After a series of clinical 
      examination and laboratory tests, there was still no clear diagnosis. Despite 
      administering diuretics and intermittent draining, the patient's condition 
      aggravated progressively. He sought further treatment at Dongzhimen Hospital 
      Respiratory Outpatient Clinic. The patient was treated with Zanthoxylum and 
      Trichosanthes Decoction (Jiao Mu Gua Lou Tang). After one and a half years, his 
      symptoms greatly improved and ultrasound revealed that the pleural effusion had 
      apparently absorbed.It is suggested that TCM herbal formulas can play a critical 
      role in preventing the progression of complicated, undiagnosed pleural effusion, 
      especially in cases of poor response to conventional therapy and thoracentesis. 
      Additional studies on the functions and mechanisms of the medicinals are 
      warranted.
