TI  - A challenging diagnosis of dyspnea: A case report of contralateral reexpansion 
      pulmonary edema.
PG  - 900
LID - 10.4081/monaldi.2018.900 [doi]
AB  - Reexpansion pulmonary edema (RPE) is an uncommon complication of thoracentesis or 
      chest drainage. It occurs in the ipsilateral or contralateral lung. Causes, 
      pathogenesis and therapy are not well understood especially for contralateral 
      RPE. We describe a case of fatal contralateral RPE in a 59-years-old woman with 
      right lung cancer underwent ultrasound-guided thoracentesis for massive pleural 
      effusion and severe dyspnea. Pathogenesis of contralateral RPE is probably 
      multifactorial and in this case is mostly due to the overperfusion of the healthy 
      lung and consequent capillary damage. The right therapy for this condition is not 
      known.
