DP  - 2016
TI  - Combined Pulmonary Fibrosis and Emphysema Preceding Lupus Pleuritis.
PG  - 783-6
LID - 10.2169/internalmedicine.55.5635 [doi]
AB  - An 83-year-old man, who was a former smoker, with anti-ribonucleoprotein (RNP) 
      antibody-positive combined pulmonary fibrosis and emphysema presented with a 
      cough and dyspnea. A chest radiograph showed bilateral pleural effusions. His 
      laboratory data showed proteinuria and elevated levels of anti-nuclear 
      antibodies, anti-double strand DNA antibodies, and CA125, with decreased serum 
      complement levels. Thoracentesis showed an exudative pleural effusion with an 
      increased lymphocyte count and elevated CA125 levels. A thoracoscopic biopsy 
      specimen showed proliferation of CA125-positive mesothelial cells. Systemic lupus 
      erythematosus was diagnosed. His symptoms and pleural effusion resolved after the 
      initiation of systemic corticosteroid therapy. The detection of anti-RNP antibody 
      and CA125 levels are helpful in the diagnosis of lupus pleuritis.
