TI  - [A rare cause of haemorrhagic pleurisy: Dissection of the aorta].
PG  - 618-21
LID - S0761-8425(16)00003-6 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.rmr.2015.11.012 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Haemorragic pleurisy is fairly common. The etiology is dominated by 
      tumors and tuberculosis. The rupture of intra-thoracic vessels into the pleural 
      cavity is a much rarer cause and the diagnosis is often delayed. OBSERVATION: A 
      77-year-old patient without previously known hypertension was hospitalized for 
      investigation of a fluid density opacity occupying the entire left hemithorax. 
      Thoracentesis revealed a non-coagulable haemorrhagic fluid. A computed tomography 
      scan showed a Stanford type B aortic dissection. The patient was given 
      anti-hypertensive treatment for one year. CONCLUSIONS: Aortic dissection remains 
      among the diagnoses to consider in the case of a haemorrhagic pleural effusion 
      despite absence of the usual suggestive symptoms.
