TI  - Pleural manometry.
PG  - 229-40
AB  - The goals of therapeutic thoracentesis are to remove the maximum amount of 
      pleural fluid to improve dyspnea and to facilitate the diagnostic evaluation of 
      large pleural effusions. Pleural manometry may be useful for immediately 
      detecting an unexpandable lung, which may coexist when any pleural fluid 
      accumulates. Pleural manometry may improve patient safety when removing large 
      amounts of pleural fluid. The basics of pleural space mechanics are discussed as 
      they apply to the normal pleural space and to pleural effusion associated with 
      expandable and unexpandable lung. This article also discusses the instrumentation 
      required to perform bedside manometry, how manometry may decrease the risk of 
      re-expansion pulmonary edema when large amounts of fluid are removed, and the 
      diagnostic capabilities of manometry.
