TI  - Thoracentesis in pericardial and pleural effusion caused by central venous 
      catheterization: a less invasive neonatal approach.
PG  - 349-51
AB  - An 840 g infant developed a rapid onset of shock-like symptoms. Pericardial and 
      pleural effusions from an indwelling central catheter were diagnosed via 
      echocardiography. A thoracentesis was promptly performed with immediate clinical 
      improvement. The fluid withdrawn from the pleural space was analysed as 
      hyperalimentation. The infant survived because of early diagnosis and aggressive 
      therapeutic intervention. A pericardial effusion should be drained if there is 
      cardiovascular compromise and because pericardiocentesis represents a high risk 
      technique, attempts should be made to rectify the extravasation via 
      thoracentesis.
