TI  - Procedural Applications of Point-of-Care Ultrasound in Pediatric Emergency 
      Medicine.
PG  - 529-554
LID - S0733-8627(21)00041-9 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.emc.2021.04.006 [doi]
AB  - Point-of-care ultrasound can improve efficacy and safety of pediatric procedures 
      performed in the emergency department. This article reviews ultrasound guidance 
      for the following pediatric emergency medicine procedures: soft tissue (abscess 
      incision and drainage, foreign body identification and removal, and peritonsillar 
      abscess drainage), musculoskeletal and neurologic (hip arthrocentesis, peripheral 
      nerve blocks, and lumbar puncture), vascular access (peripheral intravenous 
      access and central line placement), and critical care (endotracheal tube 
      placement, pericardiocentesis, thoracentesis, and paracentesis). By incorporating 
      ultrasound, emergency physicians caring for pediatric patients have the potential 
      to enhance their procedural scope, confidence, safety, and success.
