TI  - Fatal airway obstruction due to a ball-valve clot with identical signs of tension 
      pneumothorax.
PG  - 298-301
LID - 10.4266/acc.2019.00570 [doi]
AB  - Endo-tracheal tube obstruction due to an extensive blood clot is a recognized but 
      very rare complication. A ball-valve obstruction in the airway could function as 
      a check valve for the lung and thorax, resulting in tension pneumothorax-like 
      abnormalities. A 47-year-old female patient had undergone implantation of a left 
      ventricular assist device 3 weeks prior. On post-operative day 17, planned 
      thoracentesis was performed for drainage of a pleural effusion. Despite the 
      drainage, the patient's oxygenation did not improve, and emergency tracheal 
      intubation was conducted. Subsequent computed tomography revealed bilateral 
      pneumothorax. Two days later, the patient's trachea was extubated without 
      complication, and a mini-tracheostomy tube was placed. Three hours later, 
      reintubation was conducted due to progressive tachypnea. Although successful 
      intubation was confirmed, ventilation became increasingly difficult and finally 
      impossible. Marked increase in pulmonary artery and central venous pressures 
      suggested progression of the previous tension pneumothorax. After emergency 
      extracorporeal membrane oxygenation was initiated, fiberoptic bronchoscopy 
      revealed the presence of a massive clot and ball-valve obstruction of the 
      endotracheal tube. Two weeks later, the patient died due to severe hypoxic brain 
      damage. Diagnosis of ball valve clot is not simple, but intensivists should 
      consider this rare complication.
