TI  - Better Clinical Efficiency of TILs for Malignant Pleural Effusion and Ascites 
      than Cisplatin Through Intrapleural and Intraperitoneal Infusion.
PG  - 4587-4591
AB  - BACKGROUND/AIM: To evaluate the clinical efficiency of tumor-infiltrating 
      lymphocytes (TILs) compared to cisplatin for malignant pleural effusion and 
      ascites through intrapleural and intraperitoneal infusion. PATIENTS AND METHODS: 
      Thirteen patients with malignant pleural effusion and ascites were divided into a 
      TIL-treated group and a cisplatin-treated group. Patients were given TILs or 
      cisplatin, through intrapleural and intraperitoneal infusion respectively, after 
      drainage of the malignant serous effusion by thoracentesis or abdominocentesis. 
      RESULTS: The overall response rate and disease control rate of the TIL-treated 
      group (33.33% and 83.33%) were higher than that of the cisplatin-treated group 
      (28.57% and 71.43%). The progression-free survival for the TIL-treated group was 
      significantly longer (p=0.002) and better than that of the cisplatin-treated 
      group (66.67% vs. 28.57%). Quality of life apparently improved in the TIL-treated 
      group and was clearly higher than that in the cisplatin-treated group. 
      CONCLUSION: The use of TILs has a better clinical efficiency for malignant 
      pleural effusion and ascites than cisplatin through intrapleural and 
      intraperitoneal infusion without severe adverse effects.
