TI  - Conservative management of bronchopulmonary artery perforation without associated 
      haemothorax occurring at thoracentesis: a case report.
PG  - W69-72
LID - 10.1308/147870810X12699662980510 [doi]
AB  - Puncture or laceration of the pulmonary, intercostal, or peripheral vessels is an 
      uncommon, but potentially life-threatening, complication of thoracentesis, which 
      has been reported to result in severe haemothorax in 75% of patients. Damage to 
      these vessels typically requires surgical intervention or intra-arterial 
      embolisation. We report the successful non-surgical management of an unusual case 
      of pulmonary artery perforation without concomitant haemothorax in an 82-year-old 
      man who underwent thoracentesis.
