TI  - Lymphangioleiomyomatosis diagnosed by immunocytochemical and genetic analysis of 
      lymphangioleiomyomatosis cell clusters found in chylous pleural effusion.
PG  - 1593-6
AB  - A 37-year-old woman presented with a cough and discomfort in the chest. Computed 
      tomography revealed the right pleural effusion and a number of cysts in the 
      lungs. Thoracentesis revealed LAM cell clusters (LCC) in chylous pleural 
      effusion, confirmed by immunocytochemical examinations showing that the cells at 
      the center of cluster were LAM cells positive for alpha-smooth muscle actin and 
      HMB45 and the outer layer was lymphatic endothelium cells. When LCC were cultured 
      in vitro, the loss of heterozygosity of TSC2 markers was detected. This case 
      illustrates that LAM can be diagnosed by the identification of LCC without an 
      invasive biopsy if complicated with chylous effusion.
