TI  - Spontaneous bilateral haemothorax with haemopericardium secondary to rivaroxaban.
PG  - 1175-1178
LID - 10.1111/jcpt.13116 [doi]
AB  - WHAT IS KNOWN AND OBJECTIVE: Although the risk of major bleeding with non-vitamin 
      K antagonist oral anticoagulant (NOAC) is low, life-threatening bleeding can 
      occur. CASE SUMMARY: We report a case of an 81-year-old female with deep vein 
      thrombosis who developed bilateral spontaneous haemothorax and haemopericardium 
      after rivaroxaban therapy. Diagnostic thoracentesis revealed a grossly bloody 
      pleural effusion. She was treated with factor eight inhibitor bypassing agent, 
      but the result was not satisfactory. WHAT IS NEW AND CONCLUSION: To our 
      knowledge, this is the first case report of a concomitant presentation of 
      spontaneous bilateral haemothorax and haemopericardium due to rivaroxaban use. 
      This case highlights the potential risk of major haemorrhagic complication of 
      NOAC, which could be life-threatening and require emergent reversal.
