TI  - Pleural Effusion in Multiple Myeloma: Characteristics and Practice Patterns.
PG  - 69-76
LID - 10.1159/000477793 [doi]
AB  - In many Asian countries battling with the double burden of increasing 
      noninfectious diseases on top of infectious diseases, multiple myeloma (MM) 
      patients presenting with pleural effusion (PE) pose a great diagnostic challenge. 
      Thus, we aimed to analyze the clinical features and practice patterns of such 
      patients. This is a multicenter retrospective study of newly diagnosed MM 
      patients between January 2011 and December 2015. Among 575 MM patients diagnosed 
      during the study period, 80 (13.9%) that were associated with PE were identified 
      and analyzed. The most common cause of PE was parapneumonic (25%), followed by 
      reactive (18.8%). Higher CRP levels and leukocytosis were indicators of 
      parapneumonic PE. There were 7 (8.8%) with myelomatous PE and 2 (2.5%) with 
      tuberculosis. Fifty-six patients underwent additional examinations to determine 
      the exact cause of effusion; 28 patients received computed tomography (CT) of the 
      chest, 5 patients underwent thoracentesis/biopsy, and 23 patients underwent both 
      CT and thoracentesis/biopsy. On the other hand, 24 patients did not undergo 
      additional analyses but were treated empirically. Real-world analyses of practice 
      patterns in MM patients with PE showed the suboptimal use of invasive procedures 
      to determine the exact cause of PE. Since reversible causes and tuberculosis 
      pleurisy are not uncommon, invasive procedures should be actively incorporated as 
      needed.
