TI  - Transthoracic Biopsy Causes Massive Subcutaneous Emphysema in a Low Risk Patient.
PG  - TD01-TD02
LID - 10.7860/JCDR/2016/21508.8870 [doi]
AB  - Subcutaneous Emphysema (SE) can be defined as air leakage under skin from the 
      respiratory or gastrointestinal system. It is frequently accompanied by 
      pneumomediastinum. Thoracentesis, image-guided lung biopsies, pulmonary diseases 
      and therapies resulting in necrosis can cause this pathology. The risk of 
      pneumothorax and SE increased with the distance of the lesion to the pleura, and 
      small size of the lesion. Although, our patient had low risk for SE, there were 
      minimal pneumothoraces and massive SE. We consider that tumour necrosis and 
      subcutaneous tissue may be related via transthoracic biopsy and this leads to 
      massive SE.
