TI  - Does pleural fluid appearance really matter? The relationship between fluid 
      appearance and cytology, cell counts, and chemical laboratory measurements in 
      pleural effusions of patients with cancer.
PG  - 63
LID - 10.1186/1749-8090-5-63 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Previous reports have suggested that the appearance of pleural 
      effusions (i.e., the presence or absence of blood) might help to establish the 
      etiology of the effusions. This study explores the relationship between pleural 
      fluid appearance and the results of chemical and cytological analyses in a group 
      of patients with recurrent symptomatic pleural effusions and a diagnosis of 
      cancer. METHODS: Medical records were reviewed from all 390 patients who were 
      diagnosed with cancer, who underwent thoracentesis before placement of an 
      intrapleural catheter (IPC) between April 2000 and January 2006. Adequate 
      information for data analysis was available in 365 patients. The appearance of 
      their pleural fluid was obtained from procedure notes dictated by the 
      pulmonologists who had performed the thoracenteses. The patients were separated 
      into 2 groups based on fluid appearance: non-bloody and bloody. Group differences 
      in cytology interpretation were compared by using the chi square test. Cellular 
      counts, chemical laboratory results, and survival after index procedure were 
      compared by using the student's t test. RESULTS: Pleural fluid cytology was 
      positive on 82.5% of the non-bloody effusions and on 82.4% of the bloody ones. 
      The number of red blood cells (220.5 x 103/microL vs. 12.3 x 103/microL) and LDH 
      values (1914 IU/dl vs. 863 IU/dl) were statistically higher in bloody pleural 
      effusions. CONCLUSION: The presence or absence of blood in pleural effusions 
      cannot predict their etiology in patients with cancer and recurrent symptomatic 
      pleural effusions.
