TI  - [Surgical treatment of hepatocellular carcinoma with tumor thrombus in the 
      inferior vena cava].
PG  - 165-8
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To clarify the proper surgical procedure of treating hepatocellular 
      carcinoma (HCC) with a tumor thrombus in the inferior vena cava (IVC). METHODS: 
      Four patients with HCC with a tumor thrombus in the IVC underwent hepatectomy and 
      thrombectomy. Following hepatectomy, tumor thrombus was removed by incising the 
      wall of the IVC in 3 patients and from the hepatic vein in one patient. The 3 
      patients underwent thrombectomy under either hepatic vascular exclusion (HVE) or 
      Satinsky's vascular clamping. RESULTS: All the operations were successful without 
      operative death and major complications. The postoperative course was uneventful 
      in 3 patients and pleural effusion occurred in one patient who needed 
      thoracentesis. Follow-up showed 3 patients died after 30, 10 and 14 months, 
      respectively, and one patient is alive for 7 months. CONCLUSIONS: HCC with tumor 
      thrombus in the IVC is operable and the proper procedure is hepatectomy plus 
      thrombectomy.
