TI  - Amlodipine as a Suggested Cause of Yellow Nail Syndrome: A Case Report.
PG  - e29396
LID - 10.7759/cureus.29396 [doi]
LID - e29396
AB  - Yellow nail syndrome (YNS) is a rare disorder initially described in 1964. It is 
      characterized by a classical triad: yellow nails, lymphedema, and respiratory 
      manifestations. We present a 71-year-old woman who presented with progressive 
      dyspnea. Medical history includes hypertension treated with amlodipine. 
      Examination showed bilateral lower extremity non-pitting edema, yellowish 
      discoloration of nails, and bilateral pleural effusion. Thoracentesis 
      demonstrated chylous effusion. The presumptive diagnosis was YNS. Assuming 
      amlodipine as a cause of interstitial edema, it was stopped, and the symptoms 
      improved gradually. After two months, amlodipine was restarted externally, and 
      the dyspnea relapsed. Amlodipine was discontinued again. After two years of 
      amlodipine cessation, the patient remained well without symptoms. The progression 
      and resolution of symptoms point to amlodipine as a suggested cause of YNS. 
      Paying attention to the prescribed drugs was the key to diagnosing and resolving 
      serious complications.
