TI  - Myeloid sarcoma diagnosed on pleural effusion cytology: A case report and 
      literature review.
PG  - E316-E319
LID - 10.1002/dc.24739 [doi]
AB  - Myeloid sarcoma (MS) is a mass-forming, extramedullary infiltration of myeloid 
      blasts rarely presenting in cases of acute myeloid leukemia (AML). These tumoral 
      masses rarely occur at any and multiple anatomic sites, precedent or coincident 
      with bone marrow evidence of AML. We report a case of MS that presented as 
      pancreatic and cardiac masses where subsequent evaluation of pleural effusion 
      cytology rendered the diagnosis. Primary MS diagnosed via pleural effusion 
      cytology is not yet reported in literature. Herein, we report the case of a 
      45-year-old man who presented with abdominal pain. An infiltrative mass was 
      identified in the pancreatic head, suspicious for pancreatic adenocarcinoma. 
      Despite multiple attempts, Fine needle aspiration cytology of the pancreatic mass 
      failed to render a definitive diagnosis. Subsequent thoracentesis of a right 
      pleural effusion revealed cytologically malignant cells, identified as myeloid 
      blasts after immunohistochemical and flow cytometric evaluation. Although rare, 
      MS should be considered as a diagnostic possibility in the evaluation of 
      malignancy with an unknown primary.
