TI  - Advances and Controversies in Thoracentesis and Medical Thoracoscopy.
PG  - 410-416
LID - 10.1055/s-0039-1694034 [doi]
AB  - Pleural effusions are common and associated with high morbidity and mortality. 
      Whereas thoracentesis can assist in achieving a diagnosis or therapy, advances in 
      education and in the technique may prevent morbidity associated with the 
      procedure. Medical thoracoscopy is often useful for undiagnosed effusions, as 
      well as for therapeutic purposes. There is much enthusiasm about techniques for 
      biopsies that extend beyond forceps. These include biopsies using a diathermic 
      knife as well as cryoprobes. Similarly, adhesiolysis or other techniques to 
      improve therapy in multiloculated effusions using medical thoracoscopy are 
      contested. This review attempts to synthesize recent advances and controversies 
      in thoracentesis and medical thoracoscopy as clinicians head into the next decade 
      of treatment.
