VI  - 2014
DP  - 2014 Jul 9
TI  - A 33-year-old Haitian immigrant with 7 months of abdominal pain and progressive 
      distension.
LID - 10.1136/bcr-2014-205371 [doi]
LID - bcr2014205371
AB  - We report a case of a 33-year-old previously healthy Haitian immigrant with a 
      7-month history of abdominal pain, fever and ascites. He had a history of 
      positive tuberculin skin test but never underwent treatment for latent 
      tuberculosis (TB) infection. Initial examination showed abdominal distension. 
      Abdominal CT scan showed mild ascites, abnormal soft tissue in the greater 
      omentum and small bowel mesentery, retroperitoneal adenopathy, peritoneal 
      thickening and dilated loops of small bowel. Paracentesis and thoracentesis were 
      initially non-diagnostic. HIV testing was negative. The differential diagnosis 
      included lymphoma and TB peritonitis. The omental mass was biopsied under 
      ultrasound guidance, and histopathology revealed non-necrotising granulomas. 
      Sputum cultures and omental biopsy cultures subsequently grew Mycobacterium 
      tuberculosis, and a diagnosis was made of pulmonary TB with TB peritonitis. The 
      patient responded well to the initiation of anti-TB treatment.
