TI  - Effusion under the microscope.
PG  - 32-4
AB  - Pseudochylothorax is a very rare form of pleural effusion. It is also called 
      chyliform or cholesterol pleural effusion. It is usually a unilateral process and 
      approximately one-third of patients are asymptomatic at presentation. We report a 
      case of a 60 year old man with a background of rheumatoid arthritis who presented 
      with progressive dyspnea. Chest X-ray revealed a new left pleural effusion and a 
      small persistent right pleural effusion. He presented 5 years prior due to 
      recurrent pleural effusion and no diagnosis was made. Repeat thoracentesis 
      yielded 350 milliliters of thick, milky, tan-colored fluid.
