TI  - Procedural and interpretive skills of medical students: experiences and attitudes 
      of third-year students.
PG  - S48-51
AB  - BACKGROUND: Recent data do not exist on medical students' performance of and 
      attitudes toward procedural and interpretive skills deemed important by medical 
      educators. METHOD: A total of 171 medical students at seven medical schools were 
      surveyed regarding frequency of performance, self-confidence, and perceived 
      importance of 21 procedural and interpretive skills. RESULTS: Of the 122 
      responding students (71% response rate), a majority had never performed lumbar 
      puncture, thoracentesis, paracentesis, or blood culture, and students reported 
      lowest self-confidence in these skills. At least one-quarter of students had 
      never performed phlebotomy, peripheral intravenous catheter insertion, or 
      arterial blood sampling. Students perceived all 21 skills as important to learn 
      and perform during medical school. CONCLUSION: Through the third year of medical 
      school, a majority of students had never performed important procedures, and a 
      substantial minority had not performed basic procedures. Students had low 
      self-confidence in skills they rarely performed, but perceived all skills 
      surveyed as important.
