TI  - Pulmonary sarcoidosis with associated bloody pleurisy.
PG  - 1021-3
AB  - A 64-year-old man was admitted to our hospital complaining of non-productive 
      cough and right chest pain. Chest radiographs showed bilateral hilar 
      lymphadenopathy, diffuse granular nodules and right pleural effusion. Serum 
      angiotensin-II-converting enzyme and lysozyme levels were elevated. Since 
      thoracentesis indicated bloody pleurisy, video-assisted thoracoscopy was 
      performed and revealed multiple white nodules on both the visceral and parietal 
      pleura. Resected pleural biopsy specimens showed non-caseous granulomas. 
      Furthermore, some nodules were observed to compress and involve small vessels and 
      capillaries. The bloody pleurisy was assumed to have been derived from the 
      rupture of small vessels that had been compressed and affected by the granuloma 
      with sarcoidosis.
