TI  - Hemothorax and retroperitoneal hematoma after anticoagulation with enoxaparin.
PG  - 936-8
AB  - A 58-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease had spontaneous 
      bilateral hemothorax and a retroperitoneal hematoma after 4 days of 
      anticoagulation therapy with enoxaparin (1 mg/kg subcutaneously every 12 hours) 
      for suspected pulmonary thromboembolism. The patient was successfully managed 
      with red blood cell and plasma transfusions, multiple thoracenteses for 
      evacuation of blood from the pleural space, short-term mechanical ventilation, 
      and administration of bronchodilators, corticosteroids, and antibiotics. This is 
      the first report of spontaneous hemothorax and the third report of spontaneous 
      retroperitoneal hematoma associated with enoxaparin therapy.
