TI  - Giant Cavernous Hepatic Hemangioma Masking a Separate Pleural Empyema: A 
      Diagnostic Pitfall and Anchoring Bias.
PG  - e111256
LID - 10.7759/cureus.111256 [doi]
LID - e111256
AB  - Giant cavernous hepatic hemangiomas may occasionally demonstrate atypical imaging 
      characteristics that mimic malignant or infectious hepatic lesions, creating 
      diagnostic uncertainty and increasing the risk of anchoring bias. We report the 
      case of a 41-year-old male who presented with fever, pleuritic chest pain, 
      leukocytosis, and a large right pleural effusion following CT-guided lung biopsy. 
      Imaging revealed a large hepatic lesion with atypical radiographic features 
      concerning for malignancy or disseminated infection, along with pulmonary 
      inflammatory changes and empyema. Thoracentesis yielded pleural fluid concerning 
      for empyema. Given the constellation of findings, the hepatic lesion initially 
      dominated diagnostic reasoning and raised concern for a unifying metastatic or 
      infectious process. However, liver biopsy ultimately confirmed cavernous hepatic 
      hemangioma, while pulmonary biopsy demonstrated inflammatory changes without 
      evidence of malignancy. Histopathologic evaluation established that the hepatic 
      lesion was incidental and unrelated to the patient's acute illness, whereas the 
      empyema was most likely secondary to the recent thoracic intervention. This case 
      highlights the importance of avoiding anchoring bias and recognizing concurrent 
      but unrelated disease processes when evaluating complex clinical presentations.
